\chapter{Static Byte Regular Expressions and Binary Streams}
\label{ch:static-regex}

DSLtk contains two regular-expression engines, and they are not
interchangeable. The first is \api{dsl::pattern<S>}, introduced in
\chapref{ch:pattern-regex}: a compile-time predicate over a
\code{std::string_view} whose sole entry point is
\code{static constexpr bool matches (std::string_view)}. It answers exactly
one question --- \emph{does this entire string match this pattern?} --- and it
answers it at compile time, in a context where a \code{bool} is the only thing
that can be returned. The second engine is the subject of this chapter:
\api{dsl::StaticRegex<Source>}, a byte-level engine that compiles a pattern
into a Thompson non-deterministic finite automaton during constant
evaluation, and then simulates that automaton over an arbitrary buffer of
bytes at run time.

The distinction matters because the two engines answer different questions.
\code{dsl::pattern<>} cannot report \emph{where} a match occurred, because a
\code{bool} has nowhere to put an offset. It cannot search, because a search
requires a start position. It cannot match a buffer that contains a NUL byte,
because a \code{std::string_view} of type \code{char} is a text abstraction
and the pattern syntax has no way to spell a NUL. And it cannot express
alternation, grouping, optionality, or negated classes, so it cannot describe
a token grammar. Each of those omissions is a deliberate simplification for
its intended use --- compile-time dispatch on a whole string --- but each of
them is fatal when the job is to read a binary protocol.

\api{dsl::StaticRegex<>} is the answer to that second job. It operates on
\code{std::span<const std::byte>}, so all 256 byte values are ordinary
characters, NUL included. It supports the whole conventional regular
expression vocabulary short of captures: grouping, alternation, the
wildcard, character classes with ranges and negation, the three quantifiers,
the two anchors, and a small set of escapes. It reports \emph{where} a match
is, as a pair of half-open byte offsets. And because the pattern is a
template argument, the pattern is compiled by \code{constexpr} code during
translation, so a malformed pattern is a compilation error rather than a
runtime surprise. What it does not do is backtrack: the engine implements
POSIX leftmost-longest semantics by running an NFA in parallel, which means
it cannot blow up exponentially on pathological patterns, and equally that it
cannot report capture groups.

The companion facility is \api{dsl::BinaryStream}: an in-memory byte buffer
with a cursor, regex-driven token reads, explicit byte order for integers, and
integration with the abstract \api{dsl::IO} layer described in
\chapref{ch:codecs-io}. There is deliberately no length-prefixed record
helper; framing is left to the caller, because framing conventions differ
between protocols and hard-coding one would be a mistake.

\section{A worked example}

The program below tokenises a byte buffer with \code{StaticRegex<>}, then
writes and reads a length-prefixed record, then round-trips the whole thing
through an in-memory \api{dsl::IO} backend. It is complete and compiles
against \code{DSLtk.hpp} alone.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Tokenising a byte buffer and reading a length-prefixed record.},label={lst:ch32-tokenise}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <iostream>
#include <span>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace
{

// An in-memory backend for dsl::IO, so that streams can be loaded and saved
// without touching the filesystem.
struct MemoryBackend
{
  std::vector<std::byte> data;
  std::size_t offset = 0;

  std::size_t
  read (std::span<std::byte> out)
  {
    auto count = std::min (out.size (), data.size () - offset);
    std::copy_n (data.begin () + offset, count, out.begin ());
    offset += count;
    return count;
  }

  std::size_t
  write (std::span<const std::byte> bytes)
  {
    data.insert (data.end (), bytes.begin (), bytes.end ());
    return bytes.size ();
  }
};

std::string
as_text (std::span<const std::byte> bytes)
{
  std::string result;
  result.reserve (bytes.size ());
  for (auto byte : bytes)
    result += static_cast<char> (byte);
  return result;
}

std::vector<std::byte>
to_bytes (const std::string &text)
{
  std::vector<std::byte> result (text.size ());
  std::memcpy (result.data (), text.data (), text.size ());
  return result;
}

} // namespace

int
main ()
{
  using Word = dsl::StaticRegex<"[a-z]+">;
  using Digits = dsl::StaticRegex<"[0-9]+">;

  // ---- tokenise a byte buffer -----------------------------------------
  auto buffer = to_bytes ("alpha 123 beta42");

  std::cout << "longest word prefix ends at "
            << Word::prefix (buffer).value_or (0) << '\n';

  if (auto found = Digits::search (buffer))
    std::cout << "first digit run is [" << found->begin << ", " << found->end
              << ")\n";

  // Leftmost-longest, not leftmost-first: for the input "ab" the
  // alternation a|ab yields "ab" rather than "a".
  auto ab = dsl::StaticRegex<"a|ab">::search (to_bytes ("ab"));
  std::cout << "leftmost-longest alternation is [" << ab->begin << ", "
            << ab->end << ")\n";

  std::cout << "whole buffer is a word? "
            << (Word::matches (buffer) ? "yes" : "no") << '\n';
  std::cout << "\"alpha\" is a word? "
            << (Word::matches (std::string_view ("alpha")) ? "yes" : "no")
            << '\n';

  dsl::BinaryStream stream (buffer);
  std::cout << "tokens:";
  while (!stream.eof ())
    {
      if (stream.bytes[stream.pos] == std::byte { ' ' })
        {
          ++stream.pos;
          continue;
        }
      auto word = stream.next<"[a-z]+"> ();
      if (!word)
        word = stream.next<"[0-9]+"> ();
      if (word)
        std::cout << ' ' << as_text (*word);
      else
        {
          std::cout << " <bad>";
          ++stream.pos;
        }
    }
  std::cout << '\n';

  // ---- a length-prefixed record ---------------------------------------
  dsl::BinaryStream out;
  std::string payload = "payload";
  out.write_integer<std::uint32_t> (static_cast<std::uint32_t> (payload.size ()),
                                    dsl::ByteOrder::Big);
  out.write (std::as_bytes (std::span (payload.data (), payload.size ())));

  dsl::BinaryStream in (out.bytes);
  auto length = in.read_integer<std::uint32_t> (dsl::ByteOrder::Big);
  auto record = in.read (length);
  std::cout << "record (" << record.size () << " bytes): " << as_text (record)
            << '\n';

  // ---- load and save through dsl::IO ----------------------------------
  MemoryBackend backend;
  backend.data = out.bytes;
  dsl::IO<MemoryBackend> io (backend);
  auto loaded = dsl::BinaryStream::load (io);

  MemoryBackend sink;
  dsl::IO<MemoryBackend> sink_io (sink);
  loaded.save (sink_io);
  std::cout << "round trip preserved " << sink.data.size () << " bytes\n";

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch32-tokenise}.},label={lst:ch32-output}]
longest word prefix ends at 5
first digit run is [6, 9)
leftmost-longest alternation is [0, 2)
whole buffer is a word? no
"alpha" is a word? yes
tokens: alpha 123 beta 42
record (7 bytes): payload
round trip preserved 11 bytes
\end{lstlisting}

Three details in that listing are worth pausing on, because each of them is a
place where a plausible-looking program would not compile or would behave
differently than expected.

First, \code{StaticRegex<Source>} takes the pattern as its template argument,
so \code{Word} is a \emph{type}, not a value, and the three query operations
are static member functions of that type. Second, \code{BinaryStream::next}
does \emph{not} take a \code{StaticRegex} type; its template parameter is the
pattern string itself, so the call is \code{stream.next<"[a-z]+"> ()}. Writing
\code{stream.next<Word> ()} is a compile error, because \code{FixedString} and
\code{StaticRegex} are unrelated types. Third, \code{next} is \emph{anchored}
at the cursor: it does not skip over bytes that cannot begin a token. The
tokenising loop therefore has to advance past the separators itself, and it
has to try the word pattern before the digit pattern, because a pattern that
cannot match at the cursor yields an empty \code{std::optional} rather than
skipping ahead.

\section{The data model}

The engine is built from four small types in the namespace
\code{byte_regex_detail}, plus two public ones.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.27\textwidth}L{0.65\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
\code{Op} &
An eight-way tag: \code{Byte}, \code{Any}, \code{Class}, \code{Split},
\code{Empty}, \code{Begin}, \code{End}, \code{Accept}. These are the
instruction set of the automaton. \\
\code{State} &
One instruction. Holds the \code{Op}, two successor slots \code{x} and
\code{y}, a literal \code{byte} for \code{Op::Byte}, and a 256-entry
\code{chars} set for \code{Op::Class}. Successor slots hold an index into the
program or \code{-1} for none. \\
\code{Program<Capacity>} &
The compiled automaton: a fixed \code{std::array<State, Capacity>}, the number
of states actually used, the index of the start state, and a \code{valid}
flag that records whether compilation succeeded. \\
\code{Fragment} &
A partially built sub-automaton: a start state plus the list of its dangling
exits. Each exit is a \code{std::pair<int, bool>} whose \code{bool} selects
the \code{y} slot rather than the \code{x} slot of the state that owns it. \\
\code{RegexMatch} &
A public pair of half-open byte offsets, \code{begin} and \code{end}, with a
\code{size()} helper. \\
\code{StaticRegex<Source>} &
The public façade. Owns a \code{static constexpr} \code{Program} and offers
\code{prefix}, \code{search} and \code{matches}. \\
\midrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The types that make up the static byte regular expression engine.}
\label{tab:ch32-types}
\end{table}

The division of labour between \code{State::x} and \code{State::y} is worth
stating explicitly, because it explains the whole design. A \code{Split}
state uses both slots, one for each alternative. Every other state uses only
\code{x}, which is its single successor. \code{Fragment::ends} records
exactly which successor slots are still dangling and therefore need to be
patched once the next fragment's start state becomes known; the \code{bool}
in the pair says which of the two slots of that state is the dangling one.
This is the standard technique for building an automaton without any
intermediate tree: each sub-expression is compiled to a graph fragment whose
entry point is known but whose exit points are not yet known, and the exits
are patched when the fragments are joined.

\code{Program<Capacity>} is a fixed-size array rather than a vector, which is
what makes the whole thing usable as a \code{static constexpr} member: a
\code{std::vector} is not a literal type in \cstd{}, so a program stored in a
vector could not be a constant expression. The capacity is
\code{Source.view ().size () * 4 + 8}, which is a generous upper bound on the
number of states a pattern of that length can produce: each source character
contributes at most one state, each alternation bar contributes one split,
each alternative contributes one epsilon state, and the whole program
contributes one accept state.

The public API of \code{StaticRegex} is small and worth stating exactly,
because the three queries differ in a way that is easy to get wrong.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.30\textwidth}L{0.28\textwidth}L{0.34\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Call} & \textbf{Returns} & \textbf{Semantics} \\
\midrule
\code{prefix (bytes, start = 0)} &
\code{std::optional<std::size_t>} &
The offset just past the \emph{longest} match that begins exactly at
\code{start}. Fails if no match begins there. \\
\code{search (bytes, start = 0)} &
\code{std::optional<RegexMatch>} &
The \emph{leftmost-longest} match at or after \code{start}, as half-open
offsets. \\
\code{matches (bytes)} &
\code{bool} &
True if a prefix match begins at zero and ends exactly at the end of the
buffer, i.e.\ the whole buffer matches. \\
\code{matches (std::string_view)} &
\code{bool} &
The same test, with the text viewed as bytes. \\
\midrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The query surface of \code{StaticRegex}. The overload taking a
\code{std::string_view} views the text as bytes, so it inherits the byte
semantics of the whole engine.}
\label{tab:ch32-api}
\end{table}

Note that \code{prefix} returns an offset, not a range: it tells you where
the match \emph{ended}, and because it is anchored at \code{start} the range
is implicitly \code{[start, end)}. \code{search} is the only query that
reports both endpoints, which is why it is the one to reach for when
extracting a token from the middle of a buffer.

\subsection{The binary stream}

\code{BinaryStream} is deliberately thin. Its entire state is
\code{std::vector<std::byte> bytes} and \code{std::size_t pos = 0}; the
cursor is public, so a caller may inspect or adjust it directly, and this is
exactly what a hand-written framing layer needs to do.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.30\textwidth}L{0.62\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Member} & \textbf{Behaviour} \\
\midrule
\code{eof ()} &
True when the cursor is at or past the end of the buffer. \\
\code{read (count)} &
Returns a view of the next \code{count} bytes and advances the cursor.
Throws \code{std::out_of_range} with the message \code{"binary stream EOF"}
if fewer than \code{count} bytes remain. \\
\code{write (span)} &
Appends the bytes. The input is copied into a temporary vector first, so
appending a view that points into the same stream is well defined. \\
\code{next<Regex> ()} &
Applies \code{StaticRegex<Regex>::prefix} at the cursor and returns the
matched bytes as an optional view. Throws
\code{std::runtime_error} with the message
\code{"binary token regex matched empty input"} if the match is empty. \\
\code{read_integer<T> (order)} &
Reads \code{sizeof (T)} bytes and assembles an unsigned integer in the given
\code{ByteOrder}. \\
\code{write_integer<T> (value, order)} &
Appends \code{sizeof (T)} bytes in the given order. \\
\code{static load (io, limit)} &
Reads a whole stream into a new \code{BinaryStream} in 4096-byte chunks.
Throws \code{std::length_error} with the message
\code{"binary stream input limit exceeded"} if the input is larger than
\code{limit}, which defaults to 64 mebibytes. \\
\code{save (io)} &
Writes the whole buffer through \code{dsl::IO::write_all}. \\
\midrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The members of \code{BinaryStream}.}
\label{tab:ch32-stream}
\end{table}

Two aspects of this table deserve emphasis.

The first is that \code{write} copies. The comment in the header says it
plainly: staging the input so that appending a view into this stream is
well-defined. Without the copy, \code{bytes.insert (bytes.end (), input.begin
(), input.end ())} would be undefined behaviour whenever \code{input} is a
view into \code{bytes} itself, because the vector could reallocate while the
copy is in progress. This is not a hypothetical: reading a record and then
appending it to the same stream is a perfectly natural thing to want to do.

The second is that there is \emph{no} framing helper. Nothing in
\code{BinaryStream} knows about length prefixes, checksums, magic numbers or
record terminators. The header comment says only \emph{"Buffered binary
stream, with regex-token reads and explicit byte ordering."} That is the
whole contract. A protocol layer built on top has to write its own framing,
and \chapref{ch:case-binary} does exactly that.

\section{How the engine works}

The compiler is \code{Compiler<Capacity>}, and every one of its methods is a
\code{constexpr} function operating on a \code{Program}. The compilation
strategy is Thompson's construction: the pattern is parsed by recursive
descent into a small grammar, and each production of that grammar is turned
directly into automaton fragments. There is no parse tree and no intermediate
representation --- the automaton \emph{is} the parse.

The grammar has four levels. \code{expression} handles alternation,
\code{sequence} handles concatenation, \code{repeated} handles the three
quantifiers, and \code{atom} handles the terminal forms.

\begin{pseudocode}[Compiling a pattern into a Thompson NFA]
\textbf{function} compile (pattern)\\
\quad program = new Program with capacity 4 times length(pattern) plus 8\\
\quad fragment = expression()\\
\quad accept = emit(program, Accept)\\
\quad patch(program, fragment, accept)\\
\quad program.start = fragment.start\\
\quad \textbf{return} program
\end{pseudocode}

\code{compile} finishes the job by emitting the accept state and patching
every dangling exit of the top-level fragment to point at it. It then checks
that the parser consumed the entire source; if it did not, the program is
marked invalid. That final check is what turns a stray character into a
compile error rather than a silently truncated pattern.

The alternation layer is the one piece worth spelling out, because its shape
determines the shape of the resulting automaton. Each \code{expression} call
compiles its left-hand sequence, and then, for every bar it encounters, it
compiles a right-hand sequence and emits a split whose left branch is the
accumulated fragment so far and whose right branch is the new sequence. The
resulting tree is therefore left-nested: \code{a|b|c} becomes
\code{Split(Split(a, b), c)}, with the first alternative explored first.

\begin{pseudocode}[The alternation layer]
\textbf{function} expression()\\
\quad left = sequence()\\
\quad \textbf{while} the next character is a bar\\
\qquad \qquad consume the bar\\
\qquad \qquad right = sequence()\\
\qquad \qquad split = emit(program, Split(left.start, right.start))\\
\qquad \qquad left.ends = left.ends followed by right.ends\\
\qquad \qquad left.start = split\\
\quad \textbf{return} left
\end{pseudocode}

Concatenation is simpler still: \code{sequence} starts from a fresh epsilon
state, compiles each \code{repeated} in turn, and patches the previous
fragment's dangling exits to the next fragment's start state. The dangling
exits of the running result are then replaced by the dangling exits of the
newest fragment --- the exits of a concatenation are exactly the exits of its
last element.

Quantifiers are implemented with a single split state. For an atom with
fragment \code{F}, the code emits \code{Split(F.start, -1)}; for
\code{?} the split's second branch is patched to the atom's exits, so the
atom may be skipped; for \code{*} the atom's exits are patched back to the
split, forming the loop, and the split becomes the fragment's start; for
\code{+} the loop is formed identically but the fragment's start remains the
atom's start, so the atom must be taken at least once. The quantifier is
therefore a genuine loop in the automaton rather than a counted repetition,
and it is applied to one atom only: \code{a**} and \code{a*?} are both
invalid patterns.

\subsection{Atoms and escapes}

\code{atom} recognises five families of terminal. A left parenthesis
compiles a nested \code{expression} and demands a matching right parenthesis.
A left bracket compiles a character class, optionally negated by a caret,
with \code{a-z}-style ranges. A full stop becomes \code{Op::Any}. A caret
becomes \code{Op::Begin} and a dollar sign becomes \code{Op::End}, the two
anchors. Anything else is a literal byte, possibly escaped.

Escapes are handled by \code{escaped}, which recognises
\code{\n}, \code{\r}, \code{\t} and
\code{\0} for the corresponding control bytes,
\code{\x} followed by two hexadecimal digits for an arbitrary
byte value, and \emph{every other escaped character as itself}. That last
rule is the one with teeth: \code{\d} is not a digit class, it
is the letter \code{d}. The escape mechanism exists to let a pattern spell
punctuation that would otherwise be syntax --- \code{\.},
\code{\[}, \code{\*}, \code{|},
\code{(} and so on --- and to let a pattern name the control
bytes that cannot appear literally in a source file.

Character classes accumulate into the 256-entry \code{chars} set of the
class state. A negated class is implemented by inverting all 256 entries
after the class has been read, which is why \code{[^a]} matches every byte
except \code{a}, NUL included. A range whose end precedes its start is a
compile error, as is an empty class or an unterminated one.

\subsection{Simulating the automaton}

Matching does not run the automaton as a backtracking search. It runs all
reachable states in parallel, which is what produces POSIX leftmost-longest
semantics without the risk of exponential blowup. The state set is called the
\emph{active set}; between two input positions it is closed under epsilon
transitions.

\begin{pseudocode}[Simulating the NFA over a byte buffer]
\textbf{function} prefix (bytes, start)\\
\quad active = closure(program.start)\\
\quad best = none\\
\quad \textbf{for} pos from start to length(bytes)\\
\qquad \textbf{if} the accept state is in active then best = pos\\
\qquad \textbf{if} pos equals length(bytes) or active is empty then break\\
\qquad \qquad next = empty set\\
\qquad \qquad \textbf{for} each state in active\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad \textbf{if} the state consumes bytes[pos]\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad next = next joined with closure(state.next)\\
\qquad \qquad active = next\\
\quad \textbf{return} best
\end{pseudocode}

The \code{closure} helper is the epsilon part of the simulation. Starting
from a state, it follows \code{Split} transitions to both successors and
follows \code{Empty}, \code{Begin} (at offset zero) and \code{End} (at the
end of the buffer) transitions to their single successor, collecting the
states that actually consume a byte. A \code{seen} array of booleans makes
the traversal terminate even when the automaton contains a cycle, which it
does whenever a quantifier is present.

Because \code{best} is assigned on every iteration and the position
increases monotonically, the value returned is the \emph{largest} offset at
which the accept state was reachable. That is the definition of a longest
match. And because \code{search} simply tries \code{prefix} at each
successive start offset and returns the first success, the result is the
leftmost-longest match --- the POSIX rule. This is a real difference from the
Perl rule, and the difference is observable: for the input \code{ab}, the
pattern \code{a|ab} yields \code{ab} under this engine, because the automaton
explores both branches of the alternation simultaneously and the longer one
reaches the accept state at a later offset. A backtracking engine with a
greedy first alternative would return \code{a}.

\subsection{What the automaton looks like}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch32-nfa} shows the automaton that the compiler produces for
the pattern \code{a(b|c)*}, with the state indices the compiler would
actually assign. The epsilon states are artefacts of the construction --- a
sequence always begins with one, and a group compiles to its own sequence ---
but they are real states in the array and the simulation has to traverse
them.

