The practical summary is that \code{dsl::pattern<>} is a compile-time
predicate and \code{dsl::StaticRegex<>} is a run-time matcher. If you want to
ask \emph{is this whole string of the right shape?} at compile time, use the
former. If you want to know \emph{where does the next token start and end in
this buffer?} at run time, use the latter.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{warnbox}[What the engine does not do]
The static byte regular expression engine implements a regular expression
vocabulary, not a regular expression <em>language</em>. It has no captures,
no backreferences, no counted repetition with braces, no lazy or possessive
quantifiers, no named groups, no lookaround, no word boundaries, and no
shorthand classes such as \code{\d} or \code{\w}.
Attempts to use them do not silently degrade: \code{\d} matches
the letter \code{d}, and a brace is a literal brace.
\end{warnbox}

The following points are the ones that most often surprise users.

\textbf{The anchors are absolute.} \code{Op::Begin} is satisfied only when
the current offset is zero, and \code{Op::End} only when the current offset
equals the length of the whole buffer. They are not relative to the
\code{start} argument of \code{prefix} or \code{search}. Consequently
\code{StaticRegex<"^abc">::prefix (buffer, 2)} fails on a buffer that
contains \code{abc} at offset 2, because the caret is checked against offset
zero. A search that begins past the beginning of the buffer can therefore
never match a pattern containing \code{^}.

\textbf{Leftmost-longest is not leftmost-first.} As shown above, \code{a|ab}
matches \code{ab}. If you need the Perl behaviour, restructure the pattern so
that the alternatives are mutually exclusive at their first differing
character.

\textbf{Empty matches are legal but often useless.} The empty pattern
compiles to a program whose accept state is reachable at offset zero, so
\code{prefix} returns zero and \code{search} returns a zero-length match at
the first offset it tries. \code{matches} therefore accepts only the empty
string. \code{BinaryStream::next} goes one step further and throws
\code{"binary token regex matched empty input"} rather than returning a
zero-length token, because an infinite tokenising loop is almost never what
the caller wanted.

\textbf{Quantifiers bind to one atom.} \code{ab*} means \code{a} followed by
zero or more \code{b}s. To repeat a group, parenthesise it. Only one
quantifier may follow an atom: \code{a**} and \code{a*?} are compile errors,
as is a stray \code{*} or \code{?} that appears where an atom is expected.

\textbf{Character classes have sharp edges.} A right bracket must be escaped
to appear in a class, because the class ends at the first unescaped
\code{]}; an empty class is a compile error. A hyphen is a range operator
only when it sits between two members and is not immediately before the
closing bracket, so \code{[a-]} is the two-element set \code{\{a, -\}} and
\code{[-a]} likewise, while \code{[a-z]} is a range and \code{[z-a]} is a
compile error. A negated class inverts all 256 entries, so
\code{[^a]} matches NUL.

\textbf{The search cost is quadratic.} \code{search} calls \code{prefix} once
for each candidate start offset, and each call is linear in the buffer
length, so a search over an \emph{n}-byte buffer costs
\(O(n^{2} \cdot m)\) in the worst case, where \code{m} is the number of
automaton states. Each \code{prefix} call also allocates a fresh
\code{std::vector<bool>} of size \code{program.size} and a pair of state
vectors, so a search performs \emph{n} allocations. For tokenising, prefer
\code{BinaryStream::next}, which is anchored and therefore costs only
\(O(m)\) per token.

\textbf{Views into a stream are fragile.} The span returned by \code{read} or
\code{next} points into the stream's own \code{std::vector}, and the vector
may reallocate on a later \code{write}. The header documents the guarantee
precisely: views remain valid until the stream's storage changes. Copy the
bytes out if you need them to survive a write.

\textbf{Reads past the end throw.} \code{read (count)} throws
\code{std::out_of_range} with the message \code{"binary stream EOF"} rather
than returning a short span. \code{write_integer} bypasses the staging copy
and appends directly to the byte vector, which is safe because it never
aliases the buffer.

\textbf{Loading is bounded.} \code{BinaryStream::load} reads in 4096-byte
chunks and refuses to accumulate more than \code{limit} bytes, throwing
\code{std::length_error} with the message
\code{"binary stream input limit exceeded"}. The default limit is 64
mebibytes. Because the check is performed after each chunk, a stream larger
than the limit is rejected rather than truncated.

\textbf{An invalid pattern is a compile error, not a runtime one.} The
\code{static_assert} in \code{StaticRegex} fires with the message
\code{"invalid static byte regular expression"} for any pattern the compiler
rejects: an unterminated group or class, a reversed range, a stray
quantifier, trailing garbage, or a pattern so long that it exceeds the
program capacity. This is a feature --- a malformed token pattern cannot
survive into a release build --- but it does mean that a pattern built from
runtime data is not supported, because the pattern must be a
\code{FixedString} template argument.

\section{Closing}

The static byte regular expression engine is the part of DSLtk that reaches
below the level of text. It exists because binary protocols are not text
protocols: they contain NUL bytes, they need to report offsets rather than
booleans, and they need a matcher that cannot backtrack catastrophically.
By compiling the pattern during constant evaluation and running an NFA at run
time, \code{StaticRegex} delivers POSIX leftmost-longest matching over the
full 256-value byte alphabet, with a compile-time guarantee that the pattern
is well formed.

\code{BinaryStream} sits on top of it as a cursor over an in-memory buffer,
offering regex token reads, explicit byte order, and a bridge to the
\code{dsl::IO} abstraction. What it deliberately does not offer is framing:
the length-prefixed record in \lstref{lst:ch32-tokenise} is written by hand,
and that is the correct division of responsibility.

The next chapter changes the shape of the problem entirely. Instead of
matching a flat sequence of bytes against a pattern, \chapref{ch:cfg-earley}
describes how DSLtk parses a \emph{context-free grammar} with the Earley
algorithm, building an \code{ASTNode} in the process --- a language facility
that handles left recursion, epsilon productions and ambiguity that a
recursive-descent parser cannot.
